Children of the Sea
Most of the rice we eat, the clothes we wear, the medicines we need, even the phones in our
hands—almost all of it comes to us by sea. Carried across vast oceans by ships so massive they
seem like floating cities, guided by sailors whose names we may never know. This is the story 
one such sailor—Rahaat.
From childhood, Rahaat was in love with the sea. Standing on the shores of Patenga, he would
spend hours staring at the distant horizon where great ships crawled like giants across the water.
The crash of waves, the cries of seagulls, the faint glow of a lighthouse—everything pulled him
closer to the mystery of the ocean. In his heart he always wondered: What kind of life unfolds
inside those ships? Years later, after finishing his studies, Rahaat entered the marine academ
There he quickly learned that to become a sailor was not just to chase a dream but to embrace
discipline, sacrifice, and courage. Long days of training, strict rules, and endless drills mold
him into something stronger than he had ever imagined.
Finally, the day came. His first voyage began on a massive cargo ship. The ocean stretch
endlessly in every direction, a vast blue desert without borders. The air was thick with salt; the
horizon seemed unreachable. At first the sea was calm, almost gentle, and Rahaat thought he h
stepped into a world of eternal peace. But the sea does not reveal its true face so easily.
One evening, the sky darkened with alarming speed. Clouds gathered like an advancing army, and
thunder split the heavens. Within minutes the waves rose higher than houses, smashing against
the ship like a furious beast. The vessel groaned and tilted as if it were nothing more than a paper
boat. On deck, Rahaat clung to the railing, his heart racing. Rain lashed against his skin like
needles, the wind howled so loud he could barely hear himself breathe. A monstrous wave
crashed across the bow, drenching him in icy water. Fear gripped him—What if the ship sinks?
What if he never sees home again? Then, through the chaos, the captain appeared. Lightning
illuminated his stern face as he placed a firm hand on Rahaat’s shoulder and shouted above t
storm:
— “The sea terrifies us, but it also teaches us. Stand your ground—this storm will pass. A sailor
greatest strength is courage!”
Those words struck deep. Something inside Rahaat shifted. He straightened, eyes fixed on t
raging horizon. The storm roared on, but he no longer felt powerless. By dawn, the tempest faded,
and the first rays of sunlight danced on the rippling water. Exhausted yet alive, Rahaat realized 
had been reborn—no longer a boy chasing dreams, but a true sailor forged by the sea.
